   "transcript": "Woman: We live in interesting times. More and more of us like to cook at home. That’s a good thing, isn’t it? And yet, more of us live alone with nobody to cook for. I live on my own and I love to cook, so six years ago I set up a supper club and regularly invite people round to eat my food for a small fee. I’ve made new friends and shared my passion for good food. I’ve learnt a lot about how to host a dinner party and I’m gonna give you the low-down. Easy-peasy, no stress, not too much expense, how to do it.\n[Example]\nKeep it simple so you can enjoy your dinner party and don’t end up getting trapped in the kitchen. Cook what you can afford and cook what you feel like cooking. Make it hearty and homely and tasty, no mean portions either. You don’t have to impress them. You just need to feed them. They’ll be happy.\n[Question 1]\nI’m into maximalism so I always make sure there’s plenty of food. Communal dinners are a good thing. New research tells us that the key to happiness is to have a wide circle of friends, which proves that bringing people together over food is good for you. The next question.\n[Question 2]\nMix it up. Don’t just invite your best mates; invite a few randoms, your auntie, the baker, your landlord, someone you fancy. The evening will be memorable if nothing else.\n[Question 3]\nI practise what I preach. I haven’t met half these people before and they’re all new to each other. By the end of this dinner party, they’ll all have made at least one new friend.\n[Question 4]\nWhen people arrive, don’t let them stand around for too long with nothing to eat. Empty stomachs and alcohol are not a great combination. I’ve invited some Russian musicians who I only met last week, a couple of my neighbours and someone I’d only ever talked to on the phone. It adds an air of mystery to the evening. When it comes to dishing up, keep the casual theme going. Don’t do squiggles or plate graffiti or towers, and once everyone’s started eating, just kick off a good general topic of conversation.\n[Question 5]\nAnything. Booze, candles, dessert perhaps, cutlery, chairs if you haven’t got enough.\n[Question 6]\nWoman: Remember, there are dinner party givers and dinner party takers so don’t be offended if you don’t get invited back. Maybe they just haven’t got the hospitality gene.\nGuests: Sounds like a toast. To Georgia! To Georgia! To Georgia! Definitely.\nWoman: So let’s give dinner parties. You make new friends and research shows that if you cook at home, you live longer. So go for it, what’s not to like? Cheers!"
