   "transcript": "Presenter: Three hundred thousand years ago, the first full-grown humans appeared with a full-sized human brain but these Neanderthals were not like us and if evolution had stopped here, human civilisation would still be in the Stone Age. For many years archaeologist Steve Mithen has been studying the tools our ancestors left behind, trying to gain insights into how their minds worked.\nMr Mithen: So this is a small hand axe as typically made by Neanderthals. You can see some very fine flake scars where pieces of flint have been removed by striking with a hammer stone, which are excellent butchery instruments for cutting through hide, and sinews and tendons.\nPresenter: Amazing.\nMr Mithen: Yeah, they’re beautiful pieces.\nPresenter: So they really are very skilled?\nMr Mithen: They are, they’re tremendously skilful work. I mean, there’s nothing really modern humans are making which is more skilful in terms of the tool-making.\nPresenter: But Neanderthals appear to have been astonishingly unimaginative, making the same stone tools for hundreds of thousands of years.\nMr Mithen: It’s a rather little odd sort of behaviour looking at it from a modern human perspective because we normally think that the ability to make technically demanding things goes hand in hand with an ability to invent new types of tools and to innovate and to create and continue to improve on technology.\nPresenter: Steve believes that the same tunnel vision that held back the Neanderthals’ tool-making might also have restricted their linguistic abilities.\nMr Mithen: I suspect Neanderthals had words for flowers and I suspect they had words for their children, but I don’t think a Neanderthal could ever have come up with a phrase like ‘my daughter is as pretty as a flower’. I think –\nPresenter: That’s a creative thought, isn’t it?\nMr Mithen: It is in a way and what you’re doing, you’re drawing on one type of knowledge about people and children and you’re drawing on another type of knowledge about flowers, and somehow you’re making a new statement by drawing those two things together.\nPresenter: Suddenly with the arrival of modern humans, new kinds of tools appear. For the first time, these humans try using bone and antler as well as stone.\nMr Mithen: Neanderthals used bone but they didn’t carve it or manipulate it or change it at all, whereas modern humans started working bone and they carved it into harpoons and points and needles and jewellery, all sorts of things, and this really shows a big change from that tunnel vision way of thinking because now they’re taking technical skills that had been used for working wood and stone alone, and applying it to brand new material. So they’re changing what was part of an animal into something fundamentally different.\nPresenter: This revolution in tool-making marked a crucial change in mental abilities which launched us on an accelerating journey of technological development.\nMr Mithen: What seemed to happen, it snowballs so you go from a very traditional hunter-gatherer life to a world in which people are making art and clearly have religious ideologies, making very complex artefacts using body decoration, and they go through that through the Ice Age and as soon as the Ice Age finishes, people invent agriculture and we move very rapidly to towns and cities and civilisation.\nPresenter: So all this was the result of our ability to see one thing in terms of another, to think laterally. It’s a fascinating idea but we’ll never know what actual changes in the brain were required to achieve the modern human mind."
